<title>Vancomycin Dosing Trainer — PGY1</title>

<!--
==========================================================================================
  VANCOMYCIN DOSING TRAINER  —  CLINICAL SOURCE & ASSUMPTIONS BLOCK
  Read this before using the tool with a learner. Everything below is auditable in-code.
==========================================================================================

  PRIMARY GUIDELINE REFERENCE
  Rybak MJ, Le J, Lodise TP, et al. "Therapeutic monitoring of vancomycin for serious
  MRSA infections: A revised consensus guideline and review by ASHP, IDSA, PIDS, and SIDP."
  Am J Health-Syst Pharm. 2020;77(11):835-864. (Referred to throughout as "2020 consensus".)

  FIRM GUIDELINE-DERIVED NUMBERS USED HERE
  - Target AUC24/MIC 400-600 (assuming MIC = 1 mg/L by broth microdilution) for serious
    MRSA infection. Used as the grading target for every non-RRT case.
  - Trough-only monitoring targeting 15-20 mg/L is NO LONGER recommended for serious MRSA
    infection. Presented in-app as legacy practice with an explicit explanation of why.
  - Loading dose 20-35 mg/kg ACTUAL body weight in seriously ill patients.
  - AUC > 600 is associated with increased nephrotoxicity risk.
  - Vancomycin + piperacillin-tazobactam is an identified AKI risk combination.

  ASSUMPTIONS I MADE WHERE THE GUIDELINE PERMITS INSTITUTIONAL VARIATION
  (change these constants in DOSING_ASSUMPTIONS below to match your institution)
  1. LOADING DOSE CAP = 3000 mg. The guideline does not set a universal hard cap;
     2000-3000 mg is the common institutional range. 3000 mg chosen as the more permissive
     defensible default so obese cases can be taught properly.
  2. TARGET LOADING DOSE = 25 mg/kg (accepted range 20-30 mg/kg for grading; the guideline
     range extends to 35 mg/kg but doses that high are not the empiric norm on most
     general-medicine services).
  3. MAX TOTAL DAILY DOSE = 4500 mg, flagged as a safety concern above this.
  4. MAX SINGLE MAINTENANCE DOSE = 2000 mg (infusion-time and phlebitis practicality,
     not a guideline number).
  5. TROUGH TARGETS shown in the legacy panel (10-15 / 15-20 mg/L) are 2009-era numbers
     retained only for context, NOT as a recommended strategy.

  PHARMACOKINETIC MODEL USED FOR GRADING
  - CrCl by Cockcroft-Gault. Weight selection: TBW if TBW < IBW; IBW if IBW <= TBW < 1.25*IBW;
    AdjBW = IBW + 0.4*(TBW - IBW) if TBW >= 1.25*IBW. This weight-selection convention is
    standard clinical practice, NOT part of the original C-G publication.
  - Vancomycin clearance by the Matzke relationship:
        CL_vanc (mL/min) = 0.689 * CrCl (mL/min) + 3.66
    (Matzke GR, et al. Antimicrob Agents Chemother. 1984;25(4):433-437.)
    Chosen over a simple 0.75*CrCl proportionality because the non-renal intercept keeps
    low-CrCl cases from producing absurd dose recommendations.
  - AUC24 (mg*h/L) = total daily dose (mg) / CL_vanc (L/h).
    UNCERTAINTY NOTE: this is a population point-estimate. Real AUC estimation uses Bayesian
    software or two measured levels with first-order equations. The app says this explicitly
    to the learner so they do not leave thinking a population equation replaces levels.
  - Vd assumed ~0.7 L/kg for the loading-dose teaching. Not used numerically in grading.

  CASES WHERE AUC MATH IS DELIBERATELY NOT USED FOR GRADING
  Intermittent hemodialysis, CRRT, and the two non-steady-state renal cases. In these,
  population CrCl-based AUC estimation is invalid. Those cases grade on mg/kg dose bands
  and on the monitoring plan instead. Flagged per-case in the CASES array as gradeBy:'mgkg'.

  DELIBERATE DEPARTURE FROM THE ORIGINAL PROMPT SPEC — PLEASE REVIEW
  a) The brief asked for a deep-seated-infection case "requiring a more aggressive target."
     The 2020 consensus does NOT raise the AUC target for endocarditis or osteomyelitis;
     the target stays 400-600. Case 11 therefore teaches that the target does not move and
     that what changes is speed-to-target, duration, and source control. If your institution
     genuinely targets higher exposure for CNS infection, edit Case 11's teaching text.
  b) The brief asked for obesity "requiring adjusted body weight dosing." Adjusted body
     weight belongs in the CrCl ESTIMATE, not in the vancomycin dose. The dose uses actual
     body weight, but at a lower mg/kg than in a non-obese patient. Case 5 teaches that
     split explicitly because conflating the two is the most common obesity dosing error.

  SCOPE OF THE EXERCISE
  The learner is NOT asked to calculate creatinine clearance. Cockcroft-Gault is computed and
  displayed for them, along with the weight it used, so the whole exercise is the regimen:
  loading dose, maintenance dose, interval, monitoring plan. The reference panel still teaches
  the clearance calculation, because they will do it themselves on the wards.
  Where the displayed estimate is unreliable (cases 3, 6, 12) or meaningless (cases 9, 10, 13),
  the panel carries a flag saying so. Deciding what to do about an untrustworthy estimate is
  still the learner's job — the flag names the problem, not the answer.

  ENVIRONMENT
  Single file, no external dependencies, no build step, no localStorage/sessionStorage.
  All state is in-memory and resets on reload.
==========================================================================================
-->

<style>
