  crclNote:'Treat this number with suspicion. Rounding her creatinine up to 1.0 mg/dL &mdash; a common institutional convention in the frail elderly &mdash; would return about 33 mL/min instead. Both figures are estimates, not measurements.',
  aucFull:[380,620], aucPartial:[320,700],
  loading:{required:true, note:'Age is not a reason to withhold a loading dose in bacteremia.'},
  maintenance:{mode:'auc', idealText:'1,000 mg IV q24h (or 500 mg q12h). Total daily dose around 1,000 mg.'},
  intervals:{full:['24','12'], partial:['36'], note:'Either works; the daily dose is what matters.'},
  monitoring:{methodsFull:['auc_bayes','auc_2level'], timingFull:['early'], timingPartial:['ss'],
    idealText:'An early level &mdash; Bayesian estimation after the first or second dose if available &mdash; because the creatinine clearance estimate itself is the uncertain part. Waiting until steady state wastes the chance to correct sooner.'},
  reasoning:`<ol>
    <li>Her creatinine looks reassuring, but creatinine is produced by muscle. A frail 84-year-old
    with little muscle can have a genuinely low glomerular filtration rate at a creatinine of 0.6.</li>
    <li>Cockcroft-Gault at face value gives about 55 mL/min. Rounding creatinine to 1.0 gives about
    33 mL/min. Both are guesses; neither is a measurement.</li>
    <li>Give the full loading dose &mdash; 25 &times; 52 &asymp; <b>1,250 mg</b>. The loading dose does not
    depend on the clearance estimate at all, which is convenient precisely when the estimate is
    shaky.</li>
    <li>Plan the maintenance dose against the middle of the plausible range, roughly 45 mL/min:
    about 1,000 mg/day, so <b>1,000 mg q24h</b>.</li>
    <li>Then resolve the uncertainty with a level rather than with an argument about rounding
    conventions.</li></ol>`,
  pattern:`Rounding creatinine up is a convention, not a rule, and it can under-dose a septic patient just as easily as not rounding can over-dose a frail one. When the input is uncertain, the loading dose is unaffected, the maintenance dose should be planned conservatively, and the monitoring plan does the real work. Uncertainty is a reason to measure sooner, not to argue longer.`,
  errors:[
    {when:(e,c)=>e.md && (e.md*(24/(Number(e.interval)||24))) >= 1400, msg:'This total daily dose assumes her creatinine of 0.6 reflects genuinely preserved renal function. If her true clearance is closer to 33 mL/min, the same regimen produces an AUC near 800.'},
    {when:(e,c)=>e.timing==='ss', msg:'Waiting for steady state is reasonable in a stable patient with a trustworthy clearance estimate. Here the estimate is the weak link, so an early level buys you a correction two days sooner.'}
