    note:'Load 20&ndash;25 mg/kg actual body weight. She is anuric, which makes the load the only thing keeping her at target until the next session.'},
  maintenance:{mode:'mgkgdose', full:[7.0,15.0], partial:[5.5,18.0],
    idealText:'500&ndash;1,000 mg after each hemodialysis session (roughly 7.5&ndash;10 mg/kg), adjusted by pre-dialysis levels'},
  intervals:{full:['posthd'], partial:[], note:'The dose must be tied to the dialysis calendar, not to a clock interval.'},
  monitoring:{methodsFull:['auc_bayes'], methodsPartial:['trough_1520','auc_2level'], timingFull:['prehd'],
    idealText:'A pre-dialysis level before an upcoming session, used to decide the size of the post-dialysis supplemental dose.'},
  reasoning:`<ol>
    <li>Forget Cockcroft-Gault. In an anuric dialysis patient, the dialysis schedule is the
    clearance.</li>
    <li>Load 20&ndash;25 mg/kg actual body weight: about <b>1,500 mg</b>. She is anuric, so between
    now and tomorrow's session essentially nothing is being removed &mdash; but the loading dose is
    still what gets her to target today rather than on Friday.</li>
    <li>Modern high-flux dialyzers remove a meaningful fraction of vancomycin. Replace it with a
    supplemental dose <b>after each session</b>: 7.5&ndash;10 mg/kg, so roughly
    <b>500&ndash;1,000 mg post-dialysis</b>.</li>
    <li>Size that supplemental dose using a <b>pre-dialysis level</b>. This is one of the few
    settings where a concentration target rather than a calculated AUC remains standard practice,
    because AUC estimation across intermittent dialysis is not well established.</li>
    <li>Writing "1,000 mg q48h" here is a real error, not a rounding difference: on this schedule
    it drifts in and out of alignment with dialysis days, so some doses are given just before a
    session and dialysed straight back out.</li></ol>`,
  pattern:`Whenever renal replacement is involved, the first question is not "what is the CrCl" but "what removes this drug, and when." Intermittent dialysis removes it in bursts, so dose in bursts, timed to the sessions. Continuous therapy removes it continuously, so dose on a clock.`,
  errors:[
    {when:(e,c)=>['12','24','48','72','36','8'].includes(e.interval), msg:'A fixed clock interval on a Monday/Wednesday/Friday dialysis schedule will eventually put a dose immediately before a session, where much of it is removed. Anchor the dose to the dialysis run.'},
    {when:(e,c)=>e.ld===0, msg:'No loading dose. In an anuric patient the loading dose is doing almost all of the work until the next session.'}
  ]
},
{
