  loading:{required:true, note:'Critically ill and in septic shock, with an expanded volume of distribution if anything.'},
  maintenance:{mode:'mgkgday', full:[15,20], partial:[11,25],
    idealText:'750&ndash;1,000 mg IV q12h (roughly 15&ndash;20 mg/kg/day). 1,500 mg q24h is an equivalent alternative.'},
  intervals:{full:['12','24','ci'], partial:[], note:'Continuous clearance means a clock interval is appropriate here, unlike intermittent dialysis.'},
  monitoring:{methodsFull:['auc_bayes','auc_2level'], timingFull:['early'], timingPartial:['ss'],
    idealText:'A level at around 24 hours. CRRT prescriptions change &mdash; rate adjustments, filter clotting, circuit downtime &mdash; and each change alters clearance.'},
  reasoning:`<ol>
    <li>The instinct to treat an anuric patient as having near-zero clearance is exactly wrong on
    CRRT. Continuous therapy provides continuous, substantial clearance that roughly tracks the
    effluent rate.</li>
    <li>Load 25 &times; 90 &asymp; <b>2,250 mg</b>. In septic shock, volume of distribution is
