    <li>Under-dosing on CRRT is at least as common as over-dosing, and it happens for a
    psychological reason rather than a pharmacokinetic one: the patient looks anuric.</li></ol>`,
  pattern:`"Anuric" describes the kidneys, not the patient's total drug clearance. Ask what else in the room is removing the drug &mdash; a CRRT circuit, a dialyzer, an ECMO circuit &mdash; before you decide clearance is low.`,
  errors:[
    {when:(e,c)=>e.mdPerDay>0 && e.mdPerDay/c.tbw < 11, msg:'This is roughly the regimen you would write for an anuric patient <i>not</i> on renal replacement. CRRT is clearing drug continuously; the dose needs to replace what the circuit removes.'},
    {when:(e,c)=>['48','72'].includes(e.interval), msg:'An interval of 48 hours or longer assumes minimal clearance. With continuous hemodiafiltration running, concentrations fall throughout that gap.'}
