  title:'Native-valve MRSA infective endocarditis',
  age:46, sex:'M', tbw:82, heightIn:71, scr:1.0,
  vignette:`<p>A 46-year-old man who injects drugs presents with three weeks of fevers and night
    sweats. Four of four blood culture bottles grow MRSA, and a transthoracic echocardiogram shows
    a 9 mm mitral valve vegetation with mild regurgitation. He is febrile but not in heart failure
    and is not hypotensive.</p>
    <p>Creatinine is 1.0 mg/dL and stable. Cardiothoracic surgery has been consulted, and infectious
    diseases is following.</p>`,
  loading:{required:true, note:'Endocarditis with high-grade bacteremia. Getting to target on day one is the point of the load.'},
  maintenance:{mode:'auc', idealText:'1,000&ndash;1,250 mg IV q12h (total daily dose 2,000&ndash;2,500 mg)'},
  intervals:{full:['12'], partial:['8'], note:'Standard q12h at a CrCl near 100.'},
  monitoring:{methodsFull:['auc_bayes','auc_2level'], timingFull:['ss','early'],
    idealText:'AUC-guided levels, then weekly with creatinine across a six-week course. Also repeat blood cultures until clearance, and expect a transesophageal echocardiogram.'},
  reasoning:`<ol>
    <li>The intuition to be tested here is the assumption that a more severe infection deserves a
    higher target. <b>It does not.</b> The 2020 consensus target of AUC<sub>24</sub> 400&ndash;600
    applies to endocarditis exactly as it applies to a line infection.</li>
    <li>Pushing to an AUC above 600 &mdash; or to the old trough of 15&ndash;20 and beyond &mdash;
    buys nephrotoxicity without demonstrated additional efficacy. Persistent bacteremia in
    endocarditis is a source-control and organism problem far more often than a dose problem.</li>
    <li>So: CrCl &asymp; 98 mL/min, load 25 &times; 82 &asymp; <b>2,000 mg</b>, then clearance
    &asymp; 4.3 L/h, target AUC 500 &rarr; about 2,140 mg/day &rarr; <b>1,000&ndash;1,250 mg q12h</b>.</li>
    <li>What actually changes with endocarditis is everything <i>around</i> the number: six weeks of
    therapy, repeat blood cultures until they clear, transesophageal echocardiography, surgical
    evaluation, and weekly monitoring for the toxicity that a long course brings.</li></ol>`,
  pattern:`Severity of infection changes the duration, the source-control plan, and the urgency of hitting target &mdash; it does not raise the target. If your answer to a sicker patient is "aim higher," check whether any guideline actually says so before you write it.`,
  errors:[
    {when:(e,c)=>e.aucEst>c.aucPartialHi, msg:'This regimen aims above an AUC of 600. That is the reflex this case is designed to catch: the target does not rise with the severity of the infection.'},
    {when:(e,c)=>['trough_1520'].includes(e.method), msg:'Targeting a trough of 15&ndash;20 mg/L for endocarditis is 2009-era practice. The 2020 consensus specifically moved away from it because it over-exposes a large fraction of patients.'}
  ]
},
{
  n:12, level:4,
  title:'A creatinine that will not sit still',
  age:55, sex:'F', tbw:70, heightIn:66, scr:2.4,
  crclForAuc:20, crclApprox:true,
  aucFull:[330,560], aucPartial:[280,700], // tightened: the displayed CrCl overstates true clearance
  crclNote:'Shown for completeness only. Cockcroft-Gault assumes a steady state, and her creatinine is still climbing &mdash; so her true clearance is <i>lower</i> than this figure.',
  vignette:`<p>A 55-year-old woman was admitted three days ago with MRSA bacteremia from a septic
    right knee, now washed out. Over those three days her creatinine has moved 0.9 &rarr; 1.6 &rarr;
    2.4 mg/dL and her urine output has fallen to about 20 mL/h. She had a transient episode of
    hypotension on the day of surgery.</p>
    <p>She is currently normotensive on no vasopressors. She received a dose of ketorolac
    post-operatively and had CT contrast on admission.</p>`,
  loading:{required:true, note:'Still indicated, and unaffected by the renal picture.'},
  maintenance:{mode:'auc', levelGuidedOk:true,
    idealText:'Hold scheduled maintenance and redose by level. If a scheduled regimen is written, no more than 500 mg q24h with a level in 24 hours.'},
  intervals:{full:['bylevel','24'], partial:['48','36'], note:'A level-driven plan is the better answer while renal function is still moving.'},
  monitoring:{methodsFull:['auc_bayes','auc_2level'], timingFull:['early'], timingPartial:['ss'],
    idealText:'A level within about 24 hours of the loading dose, repeated as renal function declares itself. Daily creatinine.'},
  reasoning:`<ol>
    <li><b>Step two of the approach, not step three.</b> Before calculating anything, ask whether
    the creatinine is at steady state. Hers is not &mdash; it has nearly tripled in 72 hours.</li>
    <li>Cockcroft-Gault assumes creatinine production and elimination are balanced. When creatinine
    is rising, the value you are dividing by lags behind the real injury, so the equation
    <b>overestimates</b> clearance. Dosing off 25 mL/min when true clearance is closer to 10&ndash;15
    stacks drug.</li>
    <li>The loading dose is unaffected: 25 &times; 70 &asymp; <b>1,750 mg</b>. Notice that the one
    part of the regimen you can compute confidently is the part that does not need a clearance
    estimate at all.</li>
    <li>Then <b>stop and measure</b>. Hold scheduled maintenance and redose by level, or write no
    more than 500 mg q24h with a level in 24 hours. A regimen you would have to unwind tomorrow is
    worse than no regimen tonight.</li>
    <li>Also worth naming: she has taken an NSAID and received contrast, and vancomycin is now
    joining them. Consider whether an alternative MRSA agent fits better if the kidney continues to
    decline, and remove what nephrotoxins you can.</li></ol>`,
  pattern:`Every renal equation you will use assumes a steady state. Before you put a creatinine into one, look at the previous three values. If the number is moving, the equation is describing a patient who existed two days ago &mdash; load the drug, then let a measured concentration do the work the equation cannot.`,
  errors:[
    {when:(e,c)=>e.aucEst>0 && e.aucEst>=c.aucFullHi, msg:'You dosed to the creatinine clearance shown on the panel. That figure is arithmetically correct and clinically misleading: the equation needs a steady state, and hers is still deteriorating, so her real clearance is below 25 mL/min.'},
    {when:(e,c)=>e.timing==='ss', msg:'Steady state is a moving target in evolving acute kidney injury. By the time you reach the fourth dose, the clearance you dosed for no longer exists.'},
    {when:(e,c)=>e.aucEst>c.aucPartialHi, msg:'This regimen was sized for the creatinine clearance the equation reported rather than the one she is heading toward.'}
  ]
},
{
  n:13, level:4,
  title:'Septic shock with anuric acute kidney injury',
  age:73, sex:'M', tbw:88, heightIn:73, scr:3.1,
  crclApplicable:false,
  crclForAuc:10, crclApprox:true,
  aucFull:[350,650], aucPartial:[300,750],
  weights:{ld:40, maint:20, interval:15, monitor:25}, // loading dose is the whole lesson here
  crclNote:'Anuric, with a creatinine that has tripled from baseline. No clearance estimate is trustworthy here.',
  vignette:`<p>A 73-year-old man arrives from a nursing home in septic shock. He is on norepinephrine
    at 0.3 mcg/kg/min, lactate 5.2, and has been anuric for eight hours despite fluid resuscitation.
    A necrotic sacral wound is the presumed source and Gram stain shows gram-positive cocci in
    clusters. MRSA is suspected.</p>
    <p>Creatinine is 3.1 mg/dL, up from a baseline of 1.0 six weeks ago. Nephrology has been called
    and renal replacement has not yet been started.</p>`,
  loading:{required:true, note:'This is the whole case. Do not reduce it.'},
  maintenance:{mode:'auc', levelGuidedOk:true,
    idealText:'Hold scheduled maintenance after the load and redose by level. If a schedule is written, no more than about 1,000 mg q72h.'},
  intervals:{full:['bylevel','72'], partial:['48'], note:'Level-driven redosing is the correct answer in undifferentiated anuric acute kidney injury.'},
  monitoring:{methodsFull:['auc_bayes','auc_2level'], timingFull:['early'],
    idealText:'A level about 24 hours after the loading dose to decide whether and when to redose, and to re-plan the moment renal replacement starts.'},
  reasoning:`<ol>
    <li>The temptation is to reduce or skip the loading dose because he is anuric. That reasoning
    confuses the two pharmacokinetic parameters. <b>Loading dose depends on volume of distribution.
    Maintenance dose depends on clearance.</b> His clearance has collapsed; his volume of
    distribution has not &mdash; in septic shock with aggressive fluid resuscitation it is likely
    <i>larger</i> than usual.</li>
    <li>So give the full load: 25 &times; 88 = <b>2,200 mg</b>. A reduced load in a patient with
    almost no clearance is the worst of both worlds &mdash; he takes days to reach a therapeutic
    concentration, and he is in septic shock now.</li>
    <li>Then hold scheduled maintenance and redose by level. With clearance this low a single
    loading dose may hold him for two to three days.</li>
    <li>Re-plan completely the moment renal replacement starts, because that changes clearance
    abruptly and by a large factor.</li>
    <li>Worth thinking about alongside the dose: is vancomycin the right agent at all? In MRSA
    bacteremia with acute kidney injury, daptomycin is a reasonable alternative for many syndromes
    &mdash; though not for pneumonia, where surfactant inactivates it.</li></ol>`,
  pattern:`Loading dose answers "how much drug does it take to fill this person." Maintenance dose answers "how fast are they emptying." Renal failure only affects the second question. Any time you find yourself shrinking a loading dose, check which question you are actually answering.`,
  errors:[
    {when:(e,c)=>e.ld===0, msg:'No loading dose in septic shock. With clearance near zero, he will not reach a therapeutic concentration for days on maintenance dosing alone &mdash; and this is the patient who can least afford that delay.'},
    {when:(e,c)=>e.ld>0 && e.ld/c.tbw < 18, msg:'You reduced the load below 18 mg/kg because of his kidneys. Volume of distribution is unchanged, and in fluid-resuscitated septic shock it is often expanded. This is the single most common error on this case.'},
    {when:(e,c)=>['8','12','24'].includes(e.interval), msg:'A daily or more frequent schedule in an anuric patient with no renal replacement will accumulate. Redose by level instead.'}
  ]
},
